# Reawakening OS - the graphical application store.
#
# DDE Store is Debian's dde-store (1.2.5-2): a Qt 5 / DTK front end that reads
# its catalogue from AppStream and installs and removes packages through
# PackageKit. It is a store, not a desktop environment: only the DTK libraries
# and the Qt 5 platform it needs are installed, never DDE itself.
#
# The image ships a locally rebuilt dde-store package (see packaging/dde-store)
# with the upstream fix for the home page crash on AppStream categories that
# contain fewer than ten applications. Debian's 1.2.5-2 has "for (int i = 0;
# i < 10; i++)" over an arbitrarily short list; 1.2.5-2.1 fixed it upstream,
# but that release is a Qt 6 port that this Qt 5 image cannot use.
dde-store
# The Qt 5 Wayland platform plugin. libdtkgui5 depends on the Wayland *client*
# library but not on the plugin that implements the QPA platform, so without
# this the store aborts at startup: the exact failure the user hit on the
# Chromebook ("the Qt Wayland plugin was missing").
qtwayland5
# AppStream: the catalogue. appstreamcli builds /var/cache/swcatalog during the
# image build, and Debian's apt hook rebuilds it after every apt update. The
# store therefore opens with a catalogue even before any network access.
appstream
# PackageKit and its APT backend (libpk_backend_apt.so). This is the service
# the store uses to install and remove packages. It is D-Bus activated: it does
# not start at boot and exits after its idle timeout.
packagekit
# pkcon, the PackageKit diagnostic. 108 KiB, no daemon of its own, and the
# answer to "is the install path working at all?" without opening the store.
packagekit-tools
# The polkit authentication agent. PackageKit's install and remove actions ask
# polkitd for the administrator password; without a registered agent the request
# fails with "No authentication agent found" and nothing can be installed.
# mate-polkit reuses the GTK 3 stack the image already has (nm-connection-editor)
# and costs 68 KiB.
mate-polkit
# pkexec: runs the Cockpit remote-access helper through that same agent, so
# Settings → Remote can ask for a password without a terminal.
pkexec
# wl-copy: the "Copy" button next to the Cockpit address in Settings → Remote.
wl-clipboard
